#proposition[
  Let $(X', aa)$ be a measurable space, $K$ reproducing kernel for RKHS $hh_K$.
  Then every $f in hh_K$ is measurable iff $K(dot, y): X' -> RR$ is measurable for all $y in X'$.
]
#proof[\
  $==>$: #sym.checkmark\
  $<==$: Assume $K(dot, y): X' -> RR$ is measurable.
  Then every $f in H_K$ is measurable.
  For $f in hh_K$ ther is $(f_n)_(n>=1) s.t. ||f-f_n||_K -->_(n->oo) 0$.
  In particular, $f_n (x) -> f(x)$ for all $x in X'$.
  Pointwise limits of measurable functions are measurable.
]

#theorem[
  Let $(X', aa)$ be a mesaruable space, $mu$ a $sigma$-finite measure on $X'$, $hh_K$ a RKHS with kernel $K: X' times X' -> RR$.
  Let $ |K|_p := (integral_(X') |K(x,x)|^(p/2) dif mu(x))^(1/p), quad 1 <= p < oo. $
  Then
  + every $f in hh_k$ is $p$-integrable,
  + the embedding
    $ L: (hh_K, ||dot||_K) -> (L^p (X', mu), ||dot||_p), quad L(f) = f $<eq:embedding>
    is continuous with $||L|| <= |K|_p,$
  + if $1/p + 1/p' = 1$, then
    $ L^*: (L^p' (X', mu), ||dot||_(p')) -> (hh_K, ||dot||), quad (L^*g)(x) = integral_(X') g(y) K(x,y) dif mu(y) $<eq:adjoint>
    is the adjoint of $L$,
  + $hh_K$ is dense in $L^p (X', mu)$ iff $L^*$ is injective,
  + $rr(L^*)$ is dense in $hh_K$ iff $L$ is injective.
]

#proof[
  + For $f in hh_K$ we have $|f(x)| &= |chevron.l f, K_X' chevron.r_K| <= ||f||_K dot ||K_X'||_K.$
    $ integral_X' |f(x)|^p dif mu(x) &= integral_X' |chevron.l f, K_X' chevron.r_K|^p dif mu(x) <= underbrace(||f||_K^p integral_X' ||K_x||_K^p dif mu(x), integral_X' |K(x,x)|^p dif mu(x)) $
  + The above also shows this point.
  + Let $p,p' in [1,oo)$ and $1/p + 1/p' = 1$.
    Then
    $ integral_(X') |g(y)| dot |K(x,y)| dif mu(y) &<= sqrt(K(x,x)) integral_X' sqrt(K(y,y)) |g(y)| dif mu(y)\
                                                  &<= sqrt(K(x,x)) dot |K|_p dot ||g||_p' $
    Hence $X' in.rev y |-> g(y) K(dot, y)$ is integrable for every $x in X'$ and $L^* g$ is well defined.
    This shows that $g(y)K(dot,y)$ is #link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bochner_integral")[Bochner integrable] and 
    $ tilde(g) = integral_(X') g(y) K(dot, y) dif mu(y) in hh_K. $
    Consider $delta_x in hh'_K.$
    Then for every $x in X':$
    $ tilde(g)(x) &= delta_x (tilde(g))
                   = delta_x (integral_X' g(y) K(dot, y) dif mu(y))
                   = integral_X' g_9y) delta_x (K(dot, y)) dif mu(y) \
                  &= integral_X' g(y) K(x, y) dif mu(y)
                   = (L^* g)(x). $
    It remains to show that $L^*$ is the adjoint of $L$.
    #v(0.5em)
    #box(stroke: black, radius: 5pt, width: 25%, outset: 10pt)[$ A&: V -> W "linear"\ A^*&: W^* -> V^*\ &(A^* phi) (v) := phi(A v)$]
    #v(0.5em)
    To show $(L^* phi.alt)(f) = phi(L f)$ for $phi.alt in (L^p (X', mu))' tilde.eq L^p' (X', mu)$ for $p,p' in [1,oo)$ s.t. $1/p + 1/p' =1.$
    There is $g in L^p' (X', mu)$ s.t. $ phi.alt(f) = integral_X' f(x) g(x) dif mu(x).$
    $ phi.alt (L f) &= integral_X' g(y) (L f) (y) dif mu(y) = integral_X' g(y) f(y) dif mu(y)\
                    &= integral_X' overline(g)(y) chevron.l f, K_y chevron.r_K dif mu(y) = chevron.l f, integral_X' g(y) K(dot, y) dif mu(y) chevron.r_K, $
    where we were able to interchange $integral_X'$ and $chevron.l dot, dot chevron.r$ by continuity.
  + Follows from:\
    If $A in B(E,F)$, $E,F$ Banach spaces.
    Then $ "Ker"(A^*) = rr(A)^perp, quad "Ker"(A) = rr(A^*)^perp. $
  + Same as the previous point.
]

#theorem[
  Let $(X', aa)$ be a measurable space, $mu$ a $sigma$-finite measure on $X'$, and $hh_K$ separable RKHS with measurable kernel $K$, $|K|_2 < oo$.
  Then $ S_K: L^2 (X', mu) -> hh_K, quad S_K g(x) = integral_X' g(y) K(x, y) dif mu(y) $
  is a #link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert%E2%80%93Schmidt_operator")[Hilbert-Schmidt operator] #footnote[HS operators are similar to matrices in some sense.].
  Furthermore, $ T_K: L^2 (X', mu) -> L^2 (X', mu), quad T_K = S_K^* S_K $ is compact, positive, self-adjoint, and nuclear with
  $ ||T_K||_N = ||S_K||_("HS") = |K|_2. $
]

#proof[
  $S^*_K: hh_K -> L^2 (X', mu).$ Let ${v_n | n in NN}$ be an orthonormal basis for $hh_K$.
  Then $ sum_(n in NN) ||S_K^* v_n||_2^2 &= sum_(n in NN) integral_X' |S_K^* v_n (x)|^2 dif mu(x) = integral_X' sum_(n in NN) |S_K^* v_n (x)|^2 dif mu(x)\
                                         &= integral_X' sum_(n in NN)|v_n (x)|^2 dif mu(x). #footnote[Note: $S_K^*$ is the embedding.] $
  Write $ K(x, y) = sum_(n in NN) chevron.l K_x, v_n chevron.r v_n (y) = sum_(n in NN) overline(v_n (x)) v_n (y).$
  In particular, $ K(x, x) = sum_(n in NN) |v_n (x)|^2. $
  Then
  $ sum_(n in NN) ||S_K v_n||^2_2 = integral_(X') K(x,x) dif mu(x) < oo $
  due to the assumption that $|K|_2 < oo$.
  Hence $S_K^*$ is a Hilbert-Schmidt operator, and ,in particular, is compact.
  According to Theorem of Schauder: $S_K = (S_K^*)^*$ is compact.
  Since the class of compact operators forms an ideal in the space of operators, we know that $T_K = S_K^* S_K$ is also compact.
  Further, we have $T_K^* = S_K^* S_K = T_K$, i.e. $T_K$ is self-adjoint.
  $ chevron.l T_K g, g chevron.r = chevron.l S_K^* S_K g, g chevron.r = chevron.l S_K g, S_K g chevron.r = ||S_K g||_2^2 >= 0, $
  hence $T_K$ is positive.
  $ integral_X' integral_X' |K(x,y)|^2 dif mu(x) dif mu(y) <= (integral_X' K(x,x) dif mu(x))^(1/2) (integral_X' K(y,y) dif mu(y))^(1/2). $
  Therefore $K(dot, dot) in L^2 (X' times X', mu times mu)$ and we have the norm
  $ ||S_K||_("HS") = (integral_(X' times X') |K(x,y)|^2 dif (mu times mu) (x,y) )^(1/2) = ||K||_(L^2 (X' times X' , mu times mu)) $
  Let ${e_j : j in NN}$ be an orthonormal basis of $L^2 (X', mu)$.
  Then $ ||S_K||_("HS") = sum_(j in NN) ||S_K e_j||_K^2 = sum_(j in NN) chevron.l S_K e_j, S_K e_j chevron.r_K = sum_(j in NN) chevron.l T_K e_j, e_j chevron.r = tr(T_K) = ||T_K||_N $
]
It holds that $hh_K arrow.hook L^p (X', mu)$ for $1 <= p < oo$.

Let $X'$ be a topological space with topology $tau$.
#note[Question][
  Under what condition on $K$ do we have that $hh_K subset C(X')$?
]

We consider kernels with a special property:
#definition[Separately continuous kernel][
  Let $K: X' times X' -> CC$ be a positive definite kernel.
  $K$ is called separately continuous if $K_x = K(dot, x) in C(X')$ for every $x in X'$.
]
#note[Separate continuity does not imply continuity of $K: X' times X' -> CC$.]


#proposition[
  Let $(X', tau)$ be a topological space, $hh_K$ a RKHS with kernel K.
  Then
  + $K$ is bounded and separately continuous $<==>$ every $f in hh_K$ is bounded and continuous,
  + the embedding $ L: (hh_K, ||dot||_K) -> (C_b (X'), ||dot||_n) $ is continuous and $||iota|| = |K|_oo $
]

#proof[
  + Assume that $K_x in C(X')$ for all $x in X'.$#footnote[It may or may not also be necessary to assume $K in C(X' times X')$.]
    For $f in hh_K$ there is $(f_n)_(n in NN) subset H_K$ with $||f - f_n||_K -->_(n->oo) 0.$
    Then $|f(x) - f_n (x)| <= ||K_x|| ||f-f_n||_K$ for every $x in X'.$
    Hence $f in C_b (X').$
    Now let $hh_K subset C_b (X').$
    Then $K_x in C_b (X')$ for every $x in X'.$
    Now the boundedness of $K$ follows from Prop 4.9 (i) (in the missing part of the notes).
  + Follows from Prop 4.9 (ii).

]

=== The Feature Map
Let $X' != emptyset$ be a set, $H$ a Hilbert space, $Phi: X' -> H$ (Example 4.9).
$ K(x,y) = chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y) chevron.r_H $<eq:Phi_kernel> is a positive definite kernel on $X'.$
$Phi$ is called a _feature map_ and $H$ is called _feature space_.

#note[
  Every positive definite kernel can be written in the form of @eq:Phi_kernel.
  To see this, take $H = hh_K$ and $Phi_K: X' -> hh_K$.
]

The feature map establishes a pseudo-metric on $X'$:
$ d_Phi (x,y) = ||Phi(x) - Phi(y)||_H. $
$d_Phi$ is note necessarily a metric, since it could be that $d_Phi (x,y) = 0$ for some $x != y$.
If we additionally assume that $Phi$ is injective, however, then $d_Phi$ is a metric and $(X', d_Phi)$ is a metric space.
The feature map $Phi$ is a Lispchitz continuous function.

*Motivation*: $X' = [0,1] times [0,1]$
#figure(
  image("images/kernel_trick_motivation.jpeg", width: 60%),
  caption: [Kernel trick motivation.]
)

#proposition[
  Let $(X', tau)$ be a topological space, $Phi: X' -> H$ an injective feature map, and $K(x, y) = chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y) chevron.r_H.$
  The following are eqivalent:
  + $K: X' times X' -> CC$ is continuous w.r.t. $tau.$
  + $K$ is separately continuous and $X' in.rev x |-> K(x, x) in CC$ is continuous w.r.t. $tau.$
  + $Phi$ is continuous w.r.t. $tau.$
  + $iota: (X', tau) -> (X', d_Phi)$, $iota(x) = x$ is continuous.
]<prop>
#proof[
  1.$=>$2. is obvious.
  
  3.$=>$4. $d_Phi (dot, y): (X', tau) -> (X', d_Phi)$ is continuous w.r.t. $tau$, since $Phi$ is continuous.
    The set ${x in X' | d_Phi (x, y) < epsilon}$ is open w.r.t. $tau.$
    This implies that $iota$ is continuous.

  4.$=>$3. Let $x, y in X'$. Then $||Phi(x) - Phi(y)||_H = d_Phi (x, y) = d_Phi (iota(x), iota(y)).$
    Therefore, because $iota$ is continuous, so is $Phi$.
  3.$=>$1. Let $x, y, x', y' in X'$.
    Then
    $ |K(x,y) - K(x',y')| &<= |chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y) - Phi(y') chevron.r - chevron.l Phi(x) - Phi(x'), Phi(y) chevron.r|\
    &<= ||Phi(x)||_H ||Phi(y) - Phi(y')||_H + ||Phi(y)||_H ||Phi(x) - Phi(x')||_H. $
    This shows the statement.
]

#remark[
  So as to not overfit, we introduce regularization terms. One potential regularization is:
  $ rr_(L,P) (f) = integral L(x, y, f(x)) dif P(x, y) + underbrace(lambda ||f||_K + lambda ||f'||_K, "regularization term"). $
]

#proposition[
  Let $X' != emptyset$, $hh_K$ be a RKHS with kernel $K$, and canonical feature map $Phi_K: X' -> hh_K.$
  If $Phi_K (X') subset hh_K$ is compact, then $iota: hh_K -> L^oo (X')$ is compact.
]
#proof[
  $Phi_K (X') subset hh_K$ is a compact subset of a metric space.
  In particular, $Phi_K (X')$ is bounded.
  Then $ K(x,x) = |chevron.l Phi_K (x), Phi_K (x) chevron.r| <= ||Phi (x)||_K $
  Hence, $K(x,x), x in X'$ is bounded.
  Further, $(X', d_(Phi_K))$ is a compact metric space (since $Phi_K (X')$ is compact, and $Phi_K: (X', d_Phi) -> (hh_K, ||dot||_K)$ is continuous).
  Consider $C(X', RR) := C((X', d_(Phi_K)), RR).$
  Clearly, $C(X', RR) subset L^oo (X')$ because $(X', d_Phi_K)$ is compact and continuous functions on a compact set are bounded.
  For $x, y in X', f in hh_K$ we have $ |f(x) - f(y)| = |chevron.l f, Phi(x) - Phi(y) chevron.r| <= ||f||_K ||Phi(x) - Phi(y)||_K = ||f||_K d_Phi_K (x,y) $
  Hence, $f$ is Lipschitz continuous on $(X', d_Phi_K).$
  Consider $B_1 = {f in hh_K | ||f||_K <= 1}.$
  $B_1$ is uniformly equi-continuous and is bounded w.r.t. $||dot||_oo.$
  Now apply theorem of Arzela-Ascoli to find that $overline(B)_1 subset C(X', RR)$ is compact.
  This shows that $iota$ is compact.
]

#proposition[
  Let $(X', tau)$ be a separable topological space and $K in C(X' times X').$
  Then $hh_K$ is separable.
]
#proof[
  By Proposition 4.14 $K in C(X' times X')$ implies $Phi_K: X' -> hh_K, Phi_K (x) = K(dot, x).$
  Hence $Phi_K (X')$ is separable in $H_K.$
  This implies that $hh_K$ is separable.
]

#remark[
  If $(X', tau)$ is separable, and $K in C(X' times X')$, then there is a ONB ${e_n, n in NN}$ for $hh_K.$
  In particular $ Phi(x) = K(dot, x) = sum_(n=1)^oo chevron.l K(dot, x), e_n chevron.r e_n = sum_(n=1)^oo overline(e_n (x)) e_n. $
  The convergence of this series is uniform on every compact subset $cal(U) subset X'.$
]

#definition[
  Let $(X', d)$ be a compact metric space.
  A positive definite kernel $K in C(X' times X')$ is called universal if $hh_K$ is dense in $(C(X'), ||dot||_oo).$
  A kernel separates  disjoint subsets $A, B in X'$ if there is $a in RR$ and $f in hh_K$ with $f(x) > a med forall x in A$ and $f(x) < a med forall x in B$
  A kernel separates finite resp. compact subsets if it holds for $A,B$ finite or compactact.
]

#proposition[
  Let $(X', d)$ be a metric space, $K$ a universal kernel.
  Then $K$ separates compact subsets.
]
#proof[
  $A,B in X'$ be compact and $A inter B = emptyset.$
  For $x in X'$ define $ g(x) = (dist(x, B) - dist(x, A)) / (dist(x, B) + dist(x, A)), $
  where $dist(x, M) = inf_(y in M) d(x,y).$
  The $X' in.rev x |-> dist(x, M) subset RR_+$ is continuous, hence $g$ is continuous.
  Moreover $g(x) = 1 med forall x in A$, and $g(x) = -1 med forall x in B.$
  Since $K$ is universal, there is $f in hh_K$ s.t. $||f-g||_oo < -1/2.$
  Thus $f(x) > 1/2 med forall x in A$, and $f(x) < 1/2 med forall x in B.$
]

#remark[
  + The complexity of $hh_K subset C(X')$ affects the approximation properties.
    ...
    If $hh_K$ is dense in $C(X')$, then $hh_K$ contains functions of high complexity.
    Then an overfitting of data by $inf_(f in hh_K) cal(R)_(L,P)(f)$ is very likely)
    On the other hand, if $hh_K$ is too small, then $inf_(f in hh_K) cal(R)_(L,P)(f)$ will give functions which are too smooth.
  + Now assume that $K$ is universal and defined by a feature map $Phi: X' -> H.$
    Let ${x_1, ..., x_N} subset X'$ and $y_1, ..., y_N in {-1, 1}.$
    By universality, there is an $f in hh_K$ s.t. $y_i f(x_i) > 0 med forall i=1,...,N.$
    For every $f in hh_K$ there exists a $w in H$ s.t. $f(x) = chevron.l Phi(x), w chevron.r_H.$
    #proof[
      For every $f in hh_K$ there are $a_1, ..., a_M in CC$ and $y_1, ..., y_M in X'$ s.t.
      $ f &= sum_(m=1)^M overline(a)_m K(dot, y_m), med x in X' = sum_(m=1)^M overline(a)_m chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y_m) chevron.r_H = chevron.l Phi(x), underbrace(sum_(m=1)^M a_m Phi(y_m), w) chevron.r. $
      Since the inner product is continuous this also holds for $hh_K.$
    ]
    This shows that every set ${(Phi(x_1), y_1), ..., (Phi(x_N),y_N)}$ can be separated by hyperplane defined by $w$
    into subsets $ {(Phi(x_l), 1) | x_l in X'} quad "and" quad {(Phi(x_l), -1) | x_l in X'}. $
]

=== Properties of universal kernels
#proposition[
  Let $(X', d)$ be a compact metric space and $K$ universal positive definite kernel on $X'$.
  Then
  + every feature map $Phi: X' -> H$ for which $K(x, y) = chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y) chevron.r_H$ is injective,
  + $K(x, x) > 0$ for every $x in X',$
  + if $X'' subset X'$, then $K | X'' times X'$ is universal on $X'',$
  + the kernel $ K^*: X' times X' -> CC, med K^* (x,y) = (K(x,y))/((K(x,x)K(y,y))^(1/2)) $ is universal.
]
#proof[
  + Let $Phi: X' -> H$ be a feature map with $K(x,y) = chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y) chevron.r_H$.
    Take $x_0, x_1 in X', x_0 != x_1$ and assume $Phi(x_0) = Phi(x_1).$
    Since $K$ is universal, there is an $f in hh_K$ s.t. $f(x_0)<0, med f(x_1)>0$. 
    Since $f(x) = chevron.l Phi(x), w chevron.r_H$ with $w in H.$
    $ f(x_0) = chevron.l Phi(x_0), w chevron.r_H = chevron.l Phi(x_1), w chevron.r = f(x_1).$
    Thus $x_0 = x_1$, and therefore $Phi$ is injective.
  + $K(x,x) = ||Phi(x)||_H^2 >= 0$.
    If $K(x,x) = 0$, then $Phi(x) = 0.$
    There is $f in hh_K$ with $f(x) = chevron.l Phi(x), w chevron.r_H, med w in H$, with $f(x) > 0.$ 
    Hence $K(x,x) = 0$ is impossible.
  + #math.checkmark
  + According to 2.: $K(x,x) > 0 med forall x in X'.$
    Hence $K^*$ is well-defined.
    Define $alpha(x) := (K(x,x))^(-1/2).$
    If $Phi_K$ is the canonical feature map for $K$, then $alpha Phi$ is then a feature map for $K^*$ since
    $ K^* (x,y) = chevron.l (alpha Phi)(x), (alpha Phi)(y) chevron.r_H = alpha(x) alpha(y) chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y) chevron.r_H. $
    Now let $g in C(X'), med epsilon > 0$.
    $c = ||alpha||_oo < oo.$
    Since $K$ is universal, we find $f in hh_K$ with $||f - alpha^(-1) g||_oo < epsilon / c.$
    Then $||chevron.l f, alpha(dot) Phi(dot) chevron.r - g||_oo <= ||alpha||_oo ||f-alpha^(-1) g||_oo < epsilon.$
]


#proposition[
  Let $(X', d)$ be a compact metric space, $K$ a positive definite kernel with $K(x,x) > 0 med forall x in X'.$
  Suppose that $Phi: X' -> cal(l)^2 (NN), med Phi(x) = (Phi_n (x))_(n in NN)$ is an injective feature map with $K(x,y) = chevron.l Phi(x), Phi(y) chevron.r_(cal(l)^2 (NN)).$
  If $cal(A) = "span"(Phi_n, n in NN)$ is an algebra, then $K$ is univiersal.
]
#proof[
  Since $0 < K(x,x) = ||Phi(x)||_(cal(l)^2),$ ${0} subset.neq aa,$ $Phi$ is continuous by Proposition ?4.14 (probably like 4.12)?.
  Hence $Phi_n subset C(X')$ and therefore $cal(A) subset C(X').$
  $cal(A)$ is an algebra w.r.t. pointwise multiplication.
  Now let $x, y in X', x != y$, then $Phi(x) != Phi(y).$
  Thus $aa$ separate points in $X'$.
  By the Theorem of Stone-Weierstrass we have $ "cl"_(||dot||_oo) aa = C(X'). $
  Take $g in C(X')$ then there is $f in aa$ s.t. $f = sum_(j=1)^J alpha_n_j Phi_n_j$ s.t. $||f - g||_oo < epsilon.$
  Define $w_n = alpha_n_j$ if $n = n_j$ and $w_n$ = 0 otherwise.
  Then for $w = (w_n)_(n in NN) in cal(l)^2 (NN)$ and $ f(x) = chevron.l Phi(x), w chevron.r_(cal(l)^2)$ approximates $g$ arbitrarily well.
  Hence $K$ is universal.
]

#example[
