== Mercer Kernels
Let $(X', d)$ be a compact metric space and $mu$ a finite Borel measure with support $X'$.
Let $K: X' times X' -> CC$ be a positive definite kernel with $|K|_2 < oo$ and let $hh_K$ be the corresponding RKHS. 
By Theorem 4.12:
$ T_K: L^2 (mu) -> L^2 (mu), quad T_k = S_K^*S_K $
where $ S_K: L^2 (mu) -> hh_K, quad S_K g(x) = integral_X' g(y) K(x,y) dif mu(y). $
Then $T_K$ is compact, self-adjoint, and positive definite and $"spec"(T_K) = {0,lambda_1,lambda_2,...}$ with $lambda_1 >= lambda_2 >= dots >= lambda_j -->_(j->oo) 0.$
// #figure(
//   image("images/spectrum.jpeg", width: 70%),
//   caption: [The spectrum of $T_K$.]
// )
Then there is an ONB ${phi_j, j in NN}$ with $ T_K phi_j = lambda_j phi_j " for " j in NN, $
and $ T_K = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j chevron.l dot, phi_j chevron.r phi_j quad "(spectral decomposition)". $
Every $g in L^2 (mu)$ can be decomposed as $ g = g_0 + sum_(j=1)^oo chevron.l g, phi_j chevron.r phi_j, $
where $g_0 in "ker"(T_K).$
Define $psi_j := 1/lambda_j S_K phi_j$ for $j = 1,2, dots.$
Then $psi_j in hh_K$, and since $S_K^*$ is the embedding, we have $ phi_j = 1/lambda_j T_K phi_j = 1/lambda_j S_K^* S_K phi_j = 1/lambda_j S_K phi_j = psi_j. $
Hence $phi_j = psi_j$ $mu$-almost everywhere, and in particular, $phi_j in hh_K$ and
$ lambda_j lambda_k chevron.l phi_j, phi_k chevron.r_hh_K = chevron.l S_K phi_j, S_K phi_j chevron.r_hh_K = chevron.l phi_j, T_K phi_k chevron.r_(L^2 (mu)) = lambda_k chevron.l phi_j, phi_k chevron.r = lambda_k delta_(j,k). $
Therefore, ${lambda_j^(1/2) phi_j, j in NN$ is an orthonormal system for $hh_K$.

#theorem[Mercer][
  Let $(X', d)$ be a compact metric space, $mu$ a finite Borel measure with $"supp"(mu) = X'$.
  Let $A in C(X' times X')$ with $A(x,y) = overline(A(y,x)).$
  Then
  + $aa: L^2 (mu) -> L^2 (mu), med aa f(x) = integral_X' f(y) A(x,y) dif mu (y)$ is compact and self-adjoint,
  + we have $A(x,y) = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j phi_j (x) overline(phi)_j (y)$<eq:definitioni_of_aa>
    The convergence in @eq:definitioni_of_aa is absolute and uniform, provided that $"spec"(aa) minus {0} subset RR_(>0).$ 
]<thm:mercer>
#proof[
  + Let $A in C(X' times X')$, and $X'$ is compact.
    Hence $A in L^2 (mu times mu).$
    Then $aa$ is a Hilbert-Schmidt operator in part. compact.
    Since $A(x,y) = overline(A(y,x))$, it follows that $aa$ is self-adjoint.
    Hence $"spec"(aa) = {0, lambda_1, lambda_2, ...}l$
    There is $phi_j in L^2 (mu)$ s.t. $aa phi_j = lambda_j phi_j$ for $j=1,2,....$
    By the spectral decomposition of $aa$: $ aa = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j chevron.l dot, phi_j chevron.r phi_j. $
  + The systemk ${phi.alt_j (x, y) = phi_j (x) overline(phi_j (y))$ is an orthonormal system in $L^2 (X' times X', mu times mu).$
    $ sum_(j=1)^oo chevron.l A, phi.alt_j chevron.r_(L^2 (mu times mu)) phi.alt_j (x,y) $
    This converges in $L^2 (X', times X', mu times mu)$ (due to Bessel inequality)
    to a $G in L^2 (X' times X', mu times mu)$:
    $ chevron.l A, phi.alt_j chevron.r_(L^2 (mu times mu)) = integral_X' integral_X' A(x,y) phi_j (x) overline(phi_j (y)) dif mu(x) dif mu(y) = chevron.l aa phi_j, phi_j chevron.r_(L^2 (mu)) = lambda_j. $
    Consequently, $G(x,y) = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j phi_j (x) overline (phi_j (y).$
    For $f, g, L^2 (mu)$ let $F(x,y) = g(x) overline(f(y)).$
    Now $g = g_0 + sum_(j=1)^oo chevron.l g, phi_j chevron.r phi_j,$ then $chevron.l aa f, g chevron.r_(L^2 (mu)) = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j chevron.l f, phi_j chevron.r chevron.l phi_j, g chevron.r.$
    Therefore, $ chevron.l A, F chevron.r_(L^2 (mu times mu)) = chevron.l A f, g chevron.r_(L^2 (mu)) = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j chevron.l phi.alt_j, F chevron.r. $

    We see $chevron.l A - G, F chevron.r_(L^2 (mu times mu)) = 0.$
    Since ${f = g times.o overline(f) | f,g in L^2 (mu)}$ is dense in $L^2 (mu times mu).$
    Thus, $A(x,y) = G(x,y)$ for $mu times mu$-almost all $(x,y) in X' times X'.$
    In particular, $A(x,y) = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j phi_j (x) overline(phi_j) (y) $ almost everywhere.
    It remains to show that the series in @eq:definitioni_of_aa converges absolutely and uniformly if $lambda_j > 0.$
    Since $A in C(X' times X')$, we have $aa f in C(X')$ for every $f in C(X')$.
    In particular, $phi_j in C(X').$
    For $n in NN$,
    $ A_n (x, y) = A(x,y) - sum_(j=1)^n lambda_j phi_j (x) overline(phi_j (y)).$
    Then $ A_n in C(X' times X'). $ 
    $ integral_X' integral_X' A_n (x,y) f(x) overline(f(y)) dif mu(x) dif mu(y) = .... = sum_(j=n+1)^oo lambda_j chevron.l phi_j, f chevron.r chevron.l f, phi_j chevron.r >= 0. $
    Hence $A_n (x,x) >= 0 med forall x in X'.$
    We have $ sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j |phi_j (x)|^2 <= A(x,x), $
    further, $ |sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j phi_j (x) overline(phi_j (y))| <= (sum_j lambda_j |phi_j (x)|^2)^(1/2)(sum_j lambda_j |phi_j (y)|^2)^(1/2). $
    Hence $sum_j lambda_j phi_j (x) overline(phi_j (y))$ converges uniformly w.r.t all $x in X'.$
    It defines $B(x, dot) in C(X')$ for every $x in X'.$
    For $f in C(X')$ we have $ integral_X' B(x,y) f_(y) dif mu(y) = ... = aa f(x). $
    Thus $ integral_X' (B(x,y) - A(x,y))f(y) dif mu(y) = 0 $
    and $ B(x,y) = A(x,y)$ for almost all $y in X'.$
    By continuity of $A,B$ we have $A(x,y) = B(x,y)$ for all $x,y in X'.$
    Thus $ B(x,x) = A(x,x) = sum_(j=1)^oo lambda_j |phi_j (x)|^2. $
    Since the limit of $sum_(j=1)^n lambda_j |phi_j (x)|^2$ exists and is continuous.
    The theorem of Dini shows the uniform convergence of the series.
]

