= Regularization of Risk Functionals
Let $(X', aa)$ be a measurable space, $Y subset RR$ closed subset, $P$ a probability measure on $X' times Y$.
Risk functional $ rr_(L,P) (f) integral_(X' times Y) L(x,y,f(x)) dif P(x,y). $
Minimization $ inf {rr_(L,P) (f) | f in ll^0 (X', Y)} = rr_(L,P)^*. $
Bayes decision function $f_(L,P)^*: X' times Y$ is defined by $ rr_(L,P) (f_(L,P)^*) = rr_(L,P)^*. $

#note[*Problems*][
  + $ll^0 (X',Y)$ is too big to do "reasonable" analysis.
  + How to determine $f_(L,P)^*$?
  Way out: Replace $ll^0 (X',Y)$ by some suitable RKHS $hh_K.$
]

*Regularization*:
For $lambda in (0,oo)$ define $ rr_(L,P,lambda) = rr_(L,P) (f) + lambda ||f||^2_(hh_k). $
*Aim*: $ inf{rr_(L,P,lambda) (f) | f in hh_K}. $

#note[*Questions*][\
  *Q#sub("1")*: Existence: Is there $f_(L,P,lambda)^* in hh_K$ s.t. $rr_(L,P,lambda) (f^*_(L,P,lambda) = inf {rr_(L,P,lambda) (f) | f in hh_K}$?\
  *Q#sub("2")*: Uniqueness: Is $f_(L,P,lambda)^*$ uniquely determined?\
  *Q#sub("3")*: Representation: How can $f_(L,P,lambda)^*$ be represented?\
  *Q#sub("4")*: Dependence on $lambda$: How does $f_(L,P,lambda)^*$ depend on $lambda$?\
  *Q#sub("5")*: Error Analysis: What can be said about $|rr_(L,P) (f_(L,P,lambda)^*) - rr_(L,P)^*|$?
]

For data $D = ((x_1,y_1), ..., (x_n,y_n)) in (X' times Y)^N$ define the _empirical risk_:
$ rr_(L,D,lambda)(f) = rr_(L,D)(f) + lambda||f||^2_hh_K = 1/N sum_(k=1)^(N) L(x_k,y_k,f(x_k)) + lambda ||f||^2_hh_K. $
Minimization:
$ inf{rr_(L,D,lambda)(f) | f in hh_K}. $

