#definition[
  Let $E$, $F$ be Banach spaces over $RR$.
  Let $f: E -> R union {oo}$ be convex, $w in E$ with $f(w) < oo$.
  The subdifferential of $f$ at $w$ is
  $ partial f(w) = \{w' in E' | underbrace(chevron.l w'"," v-w chevron.r, w'(v-w)) <= f(v) - f(w) med forall v in E\}, $
  The subdifferential is a set of continuous linear funcitonals.
  For interpretation in $RR$ see @fig:subdifferential.
]
#figure(
  image("images/subdifferential.png", width: 70%),
  caption: [Interpretation of the subdifferential.]
)<fig:subdifferential>

#example[
  Let $E = RR$ and $f(x) = |x|.$
  Then
  $
    partial f(x) = cases({-1}"," &x<0, [-1,1]"," &x=0, {1}"," & x>0).
  $
]

#proposition[
  Let $E$ be a Banach space, $f: E -> RR union {oo}$ be convex, and $w in E$ s.t. $f(w)<oo$.
  If $f$ is continuous, then $partial f(w)$ is non-empty, convex, and weak#super("*") compact.
  If for $delta > 0$, $c >= 0$ with $|f(w)-f(v)| <= c ||v - w||$ for every $v in w + B(0,delta)$, then $||w'|| <= c$ for every $w' in partial f(w)$.
]

#proposition[
  Let $f,g: F->RR union {oo}$ be convex, $lambda >=0, A in B(E,F).$
  Then
  + $partial (lambda f)(w) = lambda partial f(w) quad forall w in F, f(w) < oo,$
  + if $f$ is continuous at $w_0 in F$ and $f(w_0),g(w_0) < oo$, then $ partial (f+g) (w) = partial f(w) + partial g(w) med forall w in F,$
  // + (Chain Rule) if $v_0 in E$ s.t. $f$ is continuous at $A v_0$ and $f(A v_0) < oo$, then $ partial (f compose A) (v) = A^* partial f(A v) quad forall v in E " s.t. " f(A v) < oo, $
  + (Chain Rule) if $v_0 in E$ s.t. $f$ is continuous at $A v_0$ and $f(A v_0) < oo$, then $ partial (f compose A) (v_0) = A^* partial f(A v_0), $
  + $f$ has a global minimum at $w in F$ if and only if $0 in partial f(w),$
  + if $f(w) < oo$ and $f$ is continuous at $w in F$, then $f$ is Gateaux differentiable at $w$ if and only if $partial f(w)$ contains only one point.
  + if $f(w)<oo$ and $f$ is continuous at $w in E$, then $partial f$ is a monotone operator, i.e. for all $v,w in E, v' in partial f(v), w' in partial f(w): chevron.l v' - w', v - w chevron.r >= 0.$
]

#proposition[
  Let $L: X' times Y times RR -> [0,oo)$ be a convex loss function, $P$ a probability measure on $X' times Y$, and for $1 <= p <= oo:$ $ rr: L^oo (X' times Y, P) -> [0,oo), quad rr(f) = integral_(X' times Y) L(x,y,f(x,y)) dif P(x,y). $
  If $rr(f) < oo$ for every $f in L^p (X' times Y, P)$, then
  $ partial rr(f) = {h in L^p' (X' times Y, P) | h(x,y) in partial L(x,y,f(x,y)) "for" P "-a.a." (x,y) in X' times Y }, wide $
  where $partial L(x,y,t)$ is the subdifferential of $L(x,y,dot).$
]<prop:five_twelve>

#theorem[Representer Theorem for $f^*_(L,P,lambda)$][
  Let $P$ be a probability measure on $X' times Y$, $L: X' times Y times RR -> [0,oo)$ be a convex $P$-integrable Nemitski loss function of order $p in [1,oo]$,
  $hh_K$ a RKHS with $K$ a measurable and bounded kernel.
  Then for every $lambda in (0,oo)$ there is $h in L^p' (X' times Y, P), med 1/p + 1/p' = 1$,
  with $h(x,y) in partial L(x,y,f^*_(L,P,lambda)(x), med (x,y) in X' times Y$ and
  $ f^*_(L,P,lambda) = - 1/(2 lambda) integral_(X' times Y) h(x,y) K(dot,y) dif P(x,y). $
]

#proof[
  $L$ convex $==>$ $L$ continuous (Why?).
  $L$ is $P$-integrable, Nemitski of order $p in [1,oo].$
  Then $ rr: L^p (X' times Y, P) -> [0,oo), quad rr f = integral_(X' times Y) L(x,y,f(x,y)) dif P(x,y) $
  is well-defined and continuous (Prop 3.7 (iii)).
  Use @prop:five_twelve
  $ partial rr(f) = {h in L^p' (X' times Y, P) | h(x,y) in partial L (x,y,f(x,y)) "for a.a." (x,y) in X' times Y } $
  Consider $ E: hh_K -> L^p (X' times Y, P), quad E f(x,y) = f(x). $
  Then
  $ ||E f||^p_p &= integral |E f(x,y)|^p dif P(x,y) = integral_(X' times Y) |f(x)|^p dif P(x,y) \
                &= integral_(X' times Y) |chevron.l f, k(dot, x) chevron.r|^p dif P(x,y) <= integral_(X' times Y) (K(x,x))^(p/2) dif P(x,y) ||f||^p_hh_K \
                &<= c ||f||^p_hh_K. $
  For $h in L^p' (X' times Y , P), med 1/p + 1/p' = 1, med f in hh_K$
  $ integral_(X' times Y) E underbrace(f(x","y), =f(x)) dif P(x,y) &= integral_(X' times Y) chevron.l f, h(x,y) K(dot, x) chevron.r_hh_K dif P(x,y) \
  &= chevron.l f, rho(integral_(X' times Y) h(x,y) K(dot,x) dif P(x,y)) chevron.r_(hh_K). $
  Thus
  $ E^*: (L^p (X' times Y, P))' -> hh_K, quad E^* h = rho(integral_(X' times Y) h(x,y) K(dot, x) dif P(x,y)). $
  The risk functional $rr_(L,P)$ is just $rr compose E.$
  The chain rule for the subdifferential gives $ partial rr_(L,P)(f) = partial (rr compose E)(f) = E^* partial (rr(E f)). $
  Consequently, $partial rr_(L,P)(f) = {rho(E^* h) | h in L^p' (X' times Y, P) and h(x,y) in partial L(x,y,f(x))}. $
  For $N(f) := ||f||^2_hh_K$, we have $N'(f) = 2 rho(f). $
  All in all, $partial rr_(L,P,lambda)(f) = 2 lambda rho(f) + partial rr_(L,P)(f)$ for every f in $hh_K$.
  The functional $rr_(L,P,lambda)$ has a minimum at $ f^*_(L,P, lambda).$
  Thus $0 in partial rr_(L,P,lambda) (f^*_(L,P,lambda))$ since $rho$ is injective the statement follows.
]

== Dependence on the regularization parameter
*Setup*: $P$ a probability measure on $X' times Y$, $L: X' times Y times RR -> [0,oo)$ a loss function, $hh_K$ a RKHS with a measurable kernel $K$.
Let $rr^*_(L,P,hh_K) = inf_(f in hh_K) rr_(L,P)(f)$.
A function $f^*_(L,P,hh_K)$ with $rr_(L,P)(f^*_(L,P,hh_K)) = rr^*_(L,P,hh_K)$ is the minimizer of $rr_(L,P)$.

#definition[Approximation error][
  Define $ A: [0,oo) -> [0,oo), med lambda |-> A(lambda) $
  with $A(lambda) = inf{rr_(L,P)(f) + lambda ||f||^2_hh_K} - rr^*_(L,P,hh_K).$
  $A$ is called _approximation error_.
]

#proposition[
  + $A$ is non-decreasing, concave, and continuous.
  + $A(0) = 0$
  + For $0 < lambda <= theta$ we have $ A(theta) / theta <= A(lambda) / lambda.$
  + for $0 <= lambda < oo$ we have $A(lambda) <= rr_(L,P)(0) - rr^*_(L,P,hh_K).$
  + $A$ is subadditive, i.e. $A(lambda + theta) <= A(lambda) + A(theta) thick forall lambda, theta in [0,oo).$
  + If $h: [0,1] -> [0,oo), med h(lambda) -> 0$ as $lambda -> 1_-$ and $A(lambda) <= lambda h(lambda)$ for every $lambda in [0,1]$, then $ A(lambda) = 0 thick forall lambda >= 0 "and" f^* = 0. $
]

#proof[
  + Use the result\
    #box(stroke: 0.1pt, outset: 4pt, width: 100%)[
      Let ${f_iota | iota in I}$ be a family of affine linear $f_iota: RR->RR.$
      Then $f(x) = sup_(iota in I) f_iota (x)$ is convex, lower-semicontinuous $f: RR->RR union {oo}.$
      The function $f: "dom"(f) -> RR$ is continuous.
    ]
    Apply this to get (i).
  + Clear.
  + Use (i) to get for $0 < lambda <= theta$
    $ A(lambda = A(lambda/theta theta + (1-lambda/theta) dot 0)
               >= lambda/theta A(theta) + (1-lambda/theta) underbrace(A(0),=0)
               = lambda/theta A(theta). $
  + Clear.
  + Let $lambda <= theta.$
    By (iii) $ A(lambda + theta) <= (lambda + theta) theta^(-1) A(theta) = lambda theta^(-1) A(theta) + A(0) <= A(lambda) + A(theta). $
  + Let $A(lambda) <= lambda h(lambda)$ for all $lambda in [0,1].$
    For $lambda->1_-$ we have $A(1) = 0.$
    Since $A(dot)$ is non-decreasing, non-negative, and concave with $A(0) = 0$, it follows $A(lambda) = 0$ for all $lambda >= 0.$
    If there is a minimizer $f^*_(P,lambda)$ of $rr_(L,P,lambda)$ for every $lambda >= 0$, then
      - $lim_(lambda->0_+) rr_(L,P,lambda)(f^*_(L,P,lambda) = rr^*_(L,P,hh_K)$.
      - $lim_(lambda->0_+) rr_(L,P) (f^*_(L,P,lambda)) = rr^*_(L,P,hh_K)$
]

#proposition[Minimizer of minimal norm][
  Let $L$ be a conve loss function, $hh_K$ a RKHS with a measurable kernel $K$, $P$ a probability measure on $X' times Y$ with $rr^*_(L,P,hh_K) < oo$.
  Let $M = {f^* in hh_K | rr_(L,P)(f^*) = rr^*_(L,P,hh_K)}$ be non-empty.
  Then there is a unique $f^* = f^*_(L,P,lambda) in M$ of minimal norm.
]<prop:5.15>

#proof[
  *Uniqueness:* Assume $f^*_1,f^*_2 in M, f^*_1 != f^*_2.$
  Moreover, $||f^*_i||_hh_K <= ||f||_hh_K$ for all $f in M$ and $i in {1,2}$.
  Since $rr_(L,P)$ is convex (from convexity of $L$), $M$ is a convex set.
  Hence $1/2 (f_1+f_2) in M.$
  From the parallelogram identity $ ||1/2 (f_1 + f_2)||^2_hh_K < 1/2(||f_1||^2_hh_K + ||f_2||^2_hh_K) = ||f_1||^2_hh_K $ we get a contradiction.\
  *Existence:* Since $L$ is convex, $rr_(L,P): hh_K -> [0,oo)$ is lower-semi continuous (by Prop 3.5).
  This implies closedness of $M$ in $hh_K.$
  Further, $ N: hh_K -> RR union {oo}, N(f) = cases(|ff|_hh_K"," &f in M, oo "," &"otherwise") $
  is lower semi-continuous and convex.
  Let $f_0 in M.$
  Then ${f in hh_K | N(f) <= ||f||_hh_K} != emptyset$ is a bounded set.
  Apply Theorem 5.3 to see that $N$ has a global minimum, which is $f^*_(L,P,hh_K).$
]

We get for $f^*_(L,P,lambda), f^*_(L,P,hh_K)$
$ ||f^*_(L,P,lambda)||_hh_K <= ||f^*_(L,P,hh_K)||_hh_K. $
To see this, use norm minimality (minimizer?) and consider $ rr_(L,P,lambda)(f^*_(L,P,hh_K)).$
If $lambda = 0$, then $ rr_(L,P,0)(f^*_(L,P,hh_K)) = rr_(L,P)(f^*_(L,P,hh_K)) <= rr_(L,P)(f) quad forall f in hh_K. $
Thus $0 dot ||f^*_(L,P,hh_K)||^2_hh_K + rr_(L,P)(f^*_(L,P,hh_K)) = inf_(f in hh_K) rr_(L,P,0)(f).$
Hence $f^*_(L,P,0) = f_(L,P,hh_K).$

#theorem[Depenndence on the regularization parameter][
  Let $P$ be a probability measure on $X' times Y$, $L$ a convex, $P$-integrable Nemitski loss function, and $hh_K$ a RKHS with an measurable kernel $K.$
  Let $(lambda_n)_(n in NN) subset (0,oo)$ with $lambda_n -->_(n->oo) lambda.$
  If $(f^*_(L,P,lambda_n))_(n in NN) subset hh_K$ is bounded, then there is $f^*_(L,P,lambda) in hh_K$ with $||f^*_(L,P,lambda_n) - f^*_(L,P,lambda)||_hh_K -->_(n->oo) 0.$
]<thm:5.16>
#proof[
  $(f^*_lambda_n)_(n in NN)$ is bounded by assumption.
  There is a subsequence $(f^*_lambda_n_k)_(k in NN)$ s.t. $f^*_lambda_n_k -->_(n -> oo)^w f^*$.
  Then $(f^*_lambda_n_k)_(k in NN)$ is bounded in norm.
  Thus there is $c > 0$ s.t. $||f^*_lambda_n_k||_hh_K -->_(k->oo) 0$since $delta_x in hh'_K$ for every $x in X'$.
  Hence, weak convergence implies pointwise convergence.
  Use Prop 3.7 to get $ rr_(L,P)(f^*_lambda_k) -> rr_(L,P)(f^*). $
  Weak convergence implies $ ||f^*||_hh_K <= liminf_(k->oo) ||f^*_lambda_n_k||_hh_K = lim_(k->oo)||f^*_lambda_n_k||_hh_K = c. $
  $A(dot)$ is continuous, hence
  $
    lambda ||f^*||_hh_K + rr_(L,P)(f^*) - rr^*_(L,P,hh_K) <= lambda c^2 + rr_(L,P)(f^*) - rr^*_(L,P,hh_K)\
    = lim_(k->oo){rr_(L,P,lambda_n_k)(f^*_lambda_n_k) - rr^*_(L,P,hh_K)} = lim_(k->oo) A(lambda_k) = A(lambda).
  $<eq:lambda_ineq>
  Hence, $f^*$ implies $rr_(L,P,lambda).$

  For
  - $lambda>0$: $f^* = f^*_lambda$ due to uniqueness (Prop 5.1).
  - $lambda=0$: $f^*$ minimizes the functional $rr_(L,P)$ over $hh_K$.
    By @eq:lambda_ineq $||f||_hh_K <= ||f^*_(L,P,hh_K)||_hh_K$.
    Since $f^*_(L,P,hh_K)$ is uniquely determined minimizer of minimal norm.
    Hence, $f^* = f^*_(L,P,hh_K) = f^*_(L,P,0)$.
  \ #math.dots.v\
  $lambda ||f^*_lambda||^2_hh_K + rr_(L,P)(f^*_lambda) - rr^*_(L,P,hh_K) = lambda c^2 + rr_(L,P)(f^*_lambda) - rr^*_(L,P,hh_K)$
  \ #math.dots.v\
  Then there is $epsilon > 0$ and a subsequence $(f^*_lambda_n_j)_(j in NN)$ s.t. $||f^*_lambda_n_j - f^*_lambda||_hh_K > epsilon > 0$ for every $j in NN.$
  Apply the argument above to $(f^*_lambda_n_j)_(j in NN.)$
  Then there is a subsequence which converges to $f^*_lambda.$
]

#corollary[
  Let $P$ be a probability measure on $X' times Y$, $L$ convex, $P$ integrable Nemitski loss function, $hh_K$ a RKHS with bounded and measurable kernel.
  Then the following are equivalent:
  1. There is a  minimizer $f^* in hh_K$ of $rr_(L,P).$
  2. The function $(0,oo) in.rev lambda |-> ||f^*_lambda||_hh_K in [0,oo)$ is bounded.
  3. There is $c > 0$ s.t. $A(lambda) <= c lambda med forall lambda >= 0.$
]<cor:5.17>

#proof[
